<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeha is a town in the Tigray region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the site of the oldest standing building in the country, the Great Temple, built around the seventh century BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> style. With other remains and Sabaean-style inscriptions, it shows close links with Saba and is thought to have been a centre of the kingdom of D'mt, a forerunner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yeha lies in the northern Central Zone of Tigray, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Its name is written yiḥa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a> and yḥʾ in Old South Arabian script, and the town likely served as the capital of the pre-Aksumite kingdom of Dʿmt. The settlement is also a monastery site, so it has been a place of worship for a very long time, under more than one faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Temple of Yeha, the oldest standing structure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, is a tower built in the Sabaean style. It has been dated, by comparison with ancient structures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, to around 700 BCE. No radiocarbon test has been made on samples from the site, but local inscriptions support the date.</p><p>David Phillipson attributes the tower's excellent preservation to two things: the care with which its builders set a level foundation firmly on uneven bedrock, and its rededication, perhaps as early as the sixth century CE, as a Christian church. Two other sites lie at Yeha. Grat Beal Gebri is a ruined complex with a portico 10 metres wide and two sets of square pillars, and a graveyard holds several rock-hewn shaft tombs, first investigated in the early 1960s. One authority has speculated that one of these tombs held a royal burial. Another believes the ancient residential area lay about a kilometre east of the modern village.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>An Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church monastery stands at Yeha, founded, according to tradition, by Abba Aftse, one of the Nine Saints. Francisco Álvares visited in 1520, calling the town Abbafaçem, and described the tower, the monastery and the church. The present church shows Aksumite architectural features and was built between 1948 and 1949. Theodore Bent and his wife Mabel explored the site briefly in February 1893. Excavations began in 1952 under the Ethiopian Institute of Archeology, were interrupted under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, and resumed in 1993 with a French team.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yeha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sabaean influence also crossed the Red Sea: in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE the Ethiopian highland kingdom of D’mt adopted Sabaean-style temples, inscriptions and gods, as the great temple at <b>Yeha</b> shows, and the Ge’ez script of Ethiopia later developed from the South Arabian alphabet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
